-- HashMap Benchmark — CLEAR @map vs C baseline
--
-- @use_zig
--
-- MEASURED RESULTS (1M insert + 1M lookup, ReleaseFast / -O3)
-- Machine: x86-64 Linux
--
--   VARIANT                 | INSERT  | LOOKUP  | TOTAL  | vs C-String
--   C (string, FNV-1a)      |  179 ms |  150 ms |  329 ms |  1.00× (baseline)
--   C (f64-key, bit-cast)   |  137 ms |   68 ms |  206 ms |  0.63×
--   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--   CLEAR String/GPA (old)  |  711 ms |  151 ms |  863 ms |  2.62×
--   CLEAR String/Split      |  705 ms |  200 ms |  905 ms |  2.75×  ← minimal gain (see below)
--   CLEAR Numeric/f64       |  266 ms |   55 ms |  321 ms |  0.98×  ← ~= C string!
--   CLEAR Numeric/i64       |  206 ms |   55 ms |  261 ms |  0.79×  ← FASTER than C string!
--
-- KEY FINDING:
--   CLEAR HashMap<Int64, V>  beats C's FNV-1a string map on insert+lookup total.
--   CLEAR HashMap<Number, V> is essentially on par with C string maps.
--   Both numeric variants are GPA-free — all memory comes from frameAlloc
--   (bump-pointer arena), so insert cost approaches malloc-free C.
--
-- WHY STRING/SPLIT DIDN'T HELP MUCH:
--   Option 1 (split allocator) was designed to save bucket-array growth via
--   frameAlloc (no GPA lock).  It helps only when bucket reallocs dominate.
--   In practice, 1M × GPA.dupe(key) (~400 ns each) is the real bottleneck —
--   key duplication accounts for ~400 ms of the 711 ms insert cost.
--   Bucket growth is ~20 GPA reallocs × a few ms each — noise by comparison.
--   TAKEAWAY: String map performance is gated by key duplication, not buckets.
--   To fully close the gap, arena key interning (keys scoped to a frame) is needed.
--
-- WHY NUMERIC IS FAST:
--   1. No key duplication — integers/floats copied by value, zero GPA allocs.
--   2. Bucket array in frameAlloc — bump-pointer growth, no GPA lock at all.
--   3. Integer hash (~2 ns) vs FNV-1a string hash (~5 ns for short keys).
--   4. 64-bit compare vs strcmp — one instruction vs a loop.
--
-- IMPLEMENTATION:
--   HashMap<V>          → std.StringHashMapUnmanaged(V)
--                           mapPut:  heapAlloc key copy + frameAlloc bucket growth
--                           mapDeinit: free key copies (heapAlloc) + deinit (frameAlloc)
--   HashMap<K, V>       → CheatLib.NumericMapType(K, V)
--     integer K         → std.AutoHashMapUnmanaged(K, V)       (Zig default)
--     float K (f64/f32) → std.HashMapUnmanaged with bit-cast context
--                           (NaN-safe: hashes/compares via @bitCast to u64)
--   All numeric maps: single frameAlloc allocator, no key copies, no GPA.
--
-- OPTIMIZATION ROADMAP (remaining):
--
--   Step A — Arena key interning for String maps.
--     When @map lifetime is scoped to a function/request, keys can be
--     bump-allocated into the frame arena alongside the bucket array.
--     Eliminates GPA entirely from the string-map hot path.
--     Expected gain: 4–5× insert speedup → string map approaches C baseline.
--
--   Step B — Integer-keyed CLEAR maps in user code.
--     Users writing HashMap<Int64, V> or HashMap<Number, V> get near-C
--     performance today with zero annotation overhead.
